WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.032
Priya’s plan defines who may enroll.

00:00:02.032 --> 00:00:06.434
Employees must be in an eligible class, typically full-time workers averaging 30 or

00:00:06.434 --> 00:00:10.837
more hours a week, and must be actively at work when coverage begins.

00:00:10.837 --> 00:00:14.223
New hires usually serve a probationary, or waiting, period before

00:00:14.223 --> 00:00:17.271
becoming eligible; federal law caps it at 90 days.

00:00:17.271 --> 00:00:20.658
When the waiting period ends, the employee has an eligibility

00:00:20.658 --> 00:00:24.044
period, usually 31 days, to enroll without evidence of insurability.

00:00:24.044 --> 00:00:27.431
An employee who misses it is a late enrollee and

00:00:27.431 --> 00:00:30.479
may have to wait for the next open enrollment.

00:00:30.479 --> 00:00:35.220
Part-time employees may be included at the employer’s option under Maryland’s small group rules.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.975
If a group plan covers family members, an

00:00:37.975 --> 00:00:40.386
employee’s spouse and children may be eligible.

00:00:40.386 --> 00:00:44.863
Federal law generally lets children stay on a parent’s plan until age 26.

00:00:44.863 --> 00:00:48.651
Maryland has a separate rule for unmarried child dependents under 25,

00:00:48.651 --> 00:00:52.439
including grandchildren of the insured on plans covered by that rule.

00:00:52.439 --> 00:00:55.883
Newborns are covered from birth and adopted children from placement.

00:00:55.883 --> 00:01:00.360
When an extra premium applies, notice and payment are due within 31 days.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:03.804
Children who cannot support themselves because of a qualifying disability

00:01:03.804 --> 00:01:06.903
may stay covered past the age limit with proof.

00:01:06.903 --> 00:01:11.380
A medical child support order can require enrollment outside the usual enrollment period.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:15.292
Once a year the plan holds an annual open enrollment, when any

00:01:15.292 --> 00:01:18.877
eligible employee may join or change coverage without a qualifying event.

00:01:18.877 --> 00:01:23.115
Between open enrollments, HIPAA’s special enrollment periods allow entry after a loss of

00:01:23.115 --> 00:01:27.352
other coverage, marriage, birth, adoption or placement, if requested within 30 days, and

00:01:27.352 --> 00:01:31.589
after a change in Medicaid or children’s health program eligibility, within 60 days.

00:01:31.589 --> 00:01:34.849
Priya’s employee who waived coverage because of her husband’s plan

00:01:34.849 --> 00:01:38.109
may join within 30 days when he loses his job.

00:01:38.109 --> 00:01:41.042
Outside these windows the plan may make a late

00:01:41.042 --> 00:01:43.650
enrollee wait, though it may not exclude conditions.

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:47.598
Group plans include the coordination of benefits provision from Lesson 23.

00:01:47.598 --> 00:01:50.829
For an employee covered under two group plans, the

00:01:50.829 --> 00:01:53.701
plan covering her as an employee is primary.

00:01:53.701 --> 00:01:57.290
For a dependent child, the birthday rule applies, or the

00:01:57.290 --> 00:02:00.879
custodial parent’s plan when parents are divorced without a decree.

00:02:00.879 --> 00:02:04.469
The secondary plan pays the remaining allowable expense up to

00:02:04.469 --> 00:02:07.699
its own benefit, so payments never exceed the bill.

00:02:07.699 --> 00:02:12.725
Employers ask about other coverage at enrollment because COB savings lower the group’s claims.

00:02:12.725 --> 00:02:17.750
An employee who conceals a spouse’s plan may have claims reprocessed and benefits recovered.

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:20.807
Every rule above also serves a defensive purpose.

00:02:20.807 --> 00:02:24.629
Waiting periods and the actively-at-work requirement stop someone from joining

00:02:24.629 --> 00:02:28.068
the payroll to get treatment for an existing problem.

00:02:28.068 --> 00:02:31.126
Limited enrollment windows prevent people from buying coverage

00:02:31.126 --> 00:02:34.183
only when sick and dropping it when well.

00:02:34.183 --> 00:02:38.005
Participation and contribution rules keep healthy employees in the pool.

00:02:38.005 --> 00:02:42.209
Employee classes must be defined by conditions of employment, not health.

00:02:42.209 --> 00:02:45.648
Dependents enroll when the employee does, or at a

00:02:45.648 --> 00:02:49.088
qualifying event, not at the moment of a diagnosis.

00:02:49.088 --> 00:02:52.145
Together these tools let the insurer skip individual

00:02:52.145 --> 00:02:54.820
underwriting and still price the group soundly.

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:57.609
A new baker starts March 1, full time.

00:02:57.609 --> 00:03:02.142
Her 60-day waiting period ends April 30; she has until May 31 to

00:03:02.142 --> 00:03:06.327
enroll herself, her husband and her two children without evidence of insurability.

00:03:06.327 --> 00:03:09.814
She enrolls on May 10 with coverage effective June 1.

00:03:09.814 --> 00:03:13.300
In September her husband loses his job and his coverage;

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:16.439
she adds him within 30 days under special enrollment.

00:03:16.439 --> 00:03:20.623
In December a daughter is born and is covered from birth; she

00:03:20.623 --> 00:03:24.458
notifies the plan within 31 days and pays the added premium.

00:03:24.458 --> 00:03:29.340
Each step follows a rule in this lesson, and none required a medical question.
